    45	## 3. Block-on-ENTRY hard prerequisite — H3 (engine fix, must land FIRST)
    46	
    47	**H3 — `snapshot_delta` under-reports spill changes** (`docs/audits/2026-05-28-6-1c-megaudit/SYNTHESIS.md:48,:114`). `set_formula` records only the **anchor cell** in the change-log (`session.rs:1267`); `run_recalc` records only `res.changed_cells` (`session.rs:800`) which holds only the anchor for spill results (`recompute.rs:447,:659`); but `write_spill` writes the FULL spill footprint (`cells.rs:731`) without bubbling its shape into a `record_changes` call. So spill targets changed/removed by `set_formula`/recalc can be **absent** from `changedCells`/`removedCells`.
    48	
    49	**Why it gates entry:** the fix **changes `WorkbookRuntime`'s return signature** (must surface spill shape into the session change-log) — too invasive for an audit-fix cycle, deliberately deferred to "6.3 binding work MUST land this engine fix as its prerequisite" (`SYNTHESIS.md:122`). Until it lands, binding `snapshot_delta`/`undo`/`redo` ships a known-broken delta path for array formulas (and undo bumps epoch → invalidates the delta cache → the consumer needs a correct delta path). **The live-grid cluster (§2b) is blocked on H3.**
    50	
    51	→ **6.3 sequencing: H3 is increment 0.** It is the only engine-internal change in 6.3 and it must ship + be audited before any of the live-grid cluster binds.
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	## 4. Filed debts to fold into 6.3 (the binding-layer machinery)
    56	
    57	These are decision-lock §3 acceptance items + 6.1C-filed MEDs. They are not optional polish — §3 makes the cancellation model and the structured-error/panic boundary **acceptance items for the binding layer**.
    58	
    59	| Id | Item | What | Anchors |
    60	|----|------|------|---------|
    61	| **M1** | `#[napi(catch_unwind)]` boundary | No `catch_unwind` anywhere → a panic across the napi `extern "C"` callback **aborts the IDE host**. Add `#[napi(catch_unwind)]` to every `Session` method + surface a `[panic] msg` Error via the FaultGuard machinery (the `EngineError::panic` constructor exists but is unused). decision-lock §3.6 mandates a panic boundary in the common API layer. | `lib.rs` (no catch_unwind); `cells.rs:720`, `recompute.rs:856` (in-path panics); `error.rs:138-141` |
    62	| **M2** | Recalc cancel honesty | `run_recalc` allocates the op-id internally + inserts `Completed` unconditionally, never checking the cancel registry; the Node wrapper holds the mutex through `recalcDirty()` so the caller gets the op-id only AFTER completion → pre-start cancel is **non-functional**, weaker than the §6.4 contract. **Decide:** (a) `start_recalc()→OperationId` + `await_recalc(op_id)` so there is a cancel window, OR (b) document the v1 reality (in-engine recalc cancel is decorative; hard-cancel is the UDF/SQL story) and align §6.4 of the contract. This is a decision-lock §3.3 **acceptance item**. | `session.rs:786-829`; `lib.rs:4353`; `session-api.md:450-456` |
    63	| **M5** | `schema_version` DTO propagation | Engine snapshot/delta/range DTOs carry `schema_version: u16` but the napi DTOs + mapper + IDE TS DTO drop it → no forward-compat versioning surfaced. Add `schemaVersion` to the napi DTOs + mapper + TS + a loader shape-check (mismatch → `unsupported_schema_version`, which exists but has no producer). decision-lock §3.7. | `dto.rs:234,:285,:329`; `lib.rs:4242-4264`; IDE `types.ts:233-312` |
    64	| **M6** | `events`/`ops` bounded retention | `ops` HashMap + `events` Vec grow unbounded (poll reads from a cursor, never drains). Build the event-ring per contract §9 (ring + `dropped` page metadata + `Event::FullResyncRequired` re-seed); prune terminal `ops` past a horizon. (`next_op_id → checked_add` already shipped.) | `session.rs:210,:217,:790,:2315`; `session-api.md:493-504` |
    65	| **M7** | Effective-extent (cross-cutting) | `Sheet::put` grows conservative bounds even on `Blank` writes; all exporters iterate `Sheet::bounds` → blank-inflated workbook ⇒ giant export. Add `Sheet::iter_effective_cells()` / `effective_value_bounds()`. **Ambiguously filed** (synthesis "tracked cross-cutting", impl-plan "6.3"). Recommend: land it WITH the persistence-binding increment (it directly affects `save`/`export` payload size) or explicitly defer to v1.5 — decide in review. | `sheet.rs:155`; csv/xlsx/.qbook exporters |
    66	| **H1** | Cross-repo IDE `parseQuantbookError` allowlist | IDE allowlist = 22 codes (grew during 6.4); engine `Session` emits ~40. Still **missing**: `invalid_version_token`, `panic`, `sheet_not_found`, `bad_cell`, `formula_parse`, `conflicting_*`, `xlsx_*`, `csv_*`, `transaction_*`, `not_implemented_in_v1_core`. Unrecognized codes silently bucket as `unknown`, defeating the V2.7 stable-code wire contract. **Needs an IDE-side commit on `feat/visualise-v1`** extending `QuantbookErrorCode` + the record, compile-enforced (the V2.9 Record invariant). | engine `session.rs:2483-2697`, `error.rs:107-141`; IDE `session.ts:775-793`, `types.ts:1523-1582` |
    67	| **L4** | IDE `WorkbookSnapshotJson.version` TS narrowing | Required-in-Rust, optional-in-TS → tighten the TS DTO to `version: Buffer`. IDE-side, low priority. | `lib.rs:916,:4262`; IDE `types.ts:300` |
    68	
    69	**Explicitly NOT 6.3** (do not mis-file): `DepShape::LazyShape #[serde(alias)]` and the Phase-1.5 overlap `debug_assert` are **6.4 perf backlog** (`impl-plan:238,:269-270`).
    70	
    71	The `EngineError::Display = "[code] message"` ↔ IDE bracket-prefix wire contract is verified-consistent on the engine side (6.1C I1); the open gap is the **consumer side = H1**.
    72	
    73	---
    74	
    75	## 5. Proposed sequencing (sub-increments, each Codex+Opus audited)
    76	
    77	> **6.3-0 — H3 spill change-log engine fix.** The only engine-internal change. Surface the spill footprint into the session change-log (touches `WorkbookRuntime` return signature). Audit, then it unblocks the live-grid cluster. *(Also land H2-style determinism if any remains — H2 already shipped.)*
    78	>
    79	> **6.3-1 — Binding-layer machinery (cross-cutting, applies to ALL methods).** M1 (`catch_unwind` + panic→`[panic]` Error on every method) + M5 (`schema_version` on the DTOs) + the M2 decision (start/await recalc OR contract alignment). Do this BEFORE binding 32 more methods so the new bindings inherit the boundary, not retrofit it.
    80	>
    81	> **6.3-2 — Bind the independent clusters over napi.** Lifecycle/read + persistence (+ M7 here) + format + structure + tables + atomic groups (batch/transaction). ~27 methods; mechanical once 6.3-1 sets the pattern. Migrate the existing IDE call-sites to the owning `Session` as each lands (the "migrate Node early to catch missing commands" mandate).
    82	>
    83	> **6.3-3 — Bind the live-grid + ops clusters** (depends on 6.3-0 + 6.3-1): `snapshot_delta`, `undo`, `redo`, `can_undo`, `can_redo`, `cancel`, `operation_status` + M6 event-ring.
    84	>
    85	> **6.3-4 — Cross-repo IDE allowlist (H1) + L4** on `feat/visualise-v1` (compile-enforced Record + union). Pairs with whichever increment first wires `Session` into an error-aware UX path.
    86	>
    87	> **6.3-5 — The golden parity matrix + closure megaudit.** See §6.
    88	
    89	---
    90	
    91	## 6. The golden parity matrix (the 6.3 exit gate)
    92	
    93	decision-lock §5 risk #1 + `session-api.md §10:745-746`: **one golden flow matrix that runs across every binding.** A single canonical script (new sheet → set values + a spill formula → recalc → snapshot + snapshot_delta → table op → batch → undo/redo → save → open → register UDF → poll events → error-path assertions on a few stable codes) executed through the adapter, asserting byte-identical DTOs + identical `EngineError` codes. Designed as a harness that **adds a binding by adding a row**, so it catches a fork the moment a second binding exists.
    94	
    95	---
    96	
    97	## 7. THE open decision for review — which bindings does v1 actually build?
    98	
    99	decision-lock §2.8 lists **WASM/Node/C/Python**, but today only Node is real (wasm/c/pyo3-session crates are empty stubs; `quantbook-py` is the UDF worker, not a session binding). Building four full adapters is a large arc; the product wedge is the IDE (Node) + Python authoring. **Proposed staging (challenge in review):**
   100	
   101	- **v1-critical:** complete the **Node** adapter (all of §2b + the §4 debts) + the golden harness (1 row). This alone retires risk #1's "migrate Node early to catch missing commands."
   102	- **v1 (product Phase 5):** a **Python** session binding sufficient for `qb.show/publish/bind` driving the sheet (beyond the 6.4 UDF registration) — likely its own sub-arc; decide PyO3-in-process vs. reuse the existing out-of-process bridge.
   103	- **v1.5 / on concrete-consumer-demand:** **WASM** (browser/embedding) and **C** (FFI for other hosts). Build the golden harness to accept them, but don't build the adapters speculatively.
   104	
   105	**Questions for Codex:** (1) Is staging WASM/C to v1.5 consistent with the canonical product plan, or does a v1 consumer require them? (2) Is the M2 cancel decision better as the `start_recalc/await_recalc` refactor (closes the §3.3 acceptance item honestly) or a documented v1 limitation (smaller, but leaves the contract aspirational)? (3) Should M7 (effective-extent) ride the persistence increment or defer to v1.5? (4) Is "bind everything over Node first, then add bindings to the matrix" the right risk-ordering, or should a second binding (even a thin one) come online before declaring the contract frozen — i.e., does a single-binding "parity" matrix actually de-risk forks?
   106	
   107	---
   108	
   109	## 8. Risks
   110	
   111	1. **Single-binding parity is not parity.** If only Node ships in v1, the matrix proves self-consistency, not cross-binding parity — the fork risk it exists to retire only truly closes when a second binding runs the same rows. Mitigation: keep the harness binding-agnostic; bring a thin Python row online early (ties to §7-Q4).
   112	2. **H3 is invasive.** Changing `WorkbookRuntime`'s return signature can ripple through every recompute caller (the 6.4B op-budget arming touched 3 such sites). Budget a focused audit for 6.3-0.
   113	3. **M1 is broad-but-mechanical** — `catch_unwind` on ~46 methods; a missed method is an abort-the-host hole. The golden harness should include a deliberate-panic row asserting `[panic]` surfaces, not aborts.
   114	4. **Cross-repo drift (H1).** The engine-emits-40 / IDE-knows-22 gap is the exact class the 6.1C megaudit's Opus-only lane caught; the closure megaudit must include an IDE-aware lane (the 6.4-3d lesson).
   115	
   116	---
   117	
   118	## 9. Definition of done (6.3 exit)
   119	
   120	- H3 shipped + audited; the live-grid cluster binds against a correct delta path.
   121	- All 32 §2b methods bound over Node; IDE call-sites migrated to the owning `Session`; the 5 §2c methods surface honest Capability errors.
   122	- M1 (panic boundary) + M5 (schema_version) shipped on every method; M2 resolved (refactor or documented); M6 event-ring; H1 IDE allowlist closed (compile-enforced); M7 + L4 resolved-or-deferred-on-record.
   123	- The golden parity matrix runs green through Node (≥1 binding row), with a deliberate-panic + error-code-stability row.
   124	- Closure megaudit (Codex + Opus + IDE-aware Opus lane) clean.
   125	- Then: **6.2 service transport** (the HTTP-vs-gRPC pick, deferred to here).

 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	//! `WorkbookSession` — the owning, single-writer engine session (Phase 6.1B inc.2).
     2	//!
     3	//! This is the *implementation* of the binding-neutral [`EngineSession`] contract
     4	//! (`crates/ql-session`, the 6.1A Codex-validated spec at `docs/api/session-api.md`).
     5	//! Where the `ql-session` crate is the type-level contract, this is the concrete
     6	//! struct that **owns** the engine state — `Workbook + OpLog + CalcgraphSession +
     7	//! PlanCache + FunctionRegistry` — and threads a per-edit [`WorkbookRuntime`]
     8	//! through it so no borrow crosses an FFI boundary (GAP-PS-09; the
     9	//! `calcgraph_session.rs:69-71` "absorb into ONE owning struct" anticipation).
    10	//!
    11	//! ## Scope (6.1B inc.2 — core path + read/structure/table + snapshot_delta)
    12	//!
    13	//! Shipped **real**:
    14	//! - lifecycle (`new`/`close`/state gating, the `Busy` state, version `epoch`),
    15	//! - single-cell mutations (`set_value`/`set_formula`/`clear`/`set_format`/
    16	//!   `register_format`/`validate_formula`) + sheet `add`/`rename`/`delete`/
    17	//!   `restore`/`move` + `set_name` + table ops (`create`/`rename`/
    18	//!   `rename_column`/`resize`/`drop`),
    19	//! - recalc (`recalc_dirty`/`recalc_all`/`mark_volatiles_dirty`) under the
    20	//!   `Busy` + operation-registry model,
    21	//! - the read path (`snapshot`/`cell`/`list_sheets`/`query_range`) + the
    22	//!   `{epoch, state_seq}` version token + **`snapshot_delta`** (inc.2c-3 —
    23	//!   change-log design (a)),
    24	//! - operations + events (`cancel`/`operation_status`/`poll_events`).
    25	//!
    26	//! `batch` (inc.2c-4) + the multi-call **transaction handle** (`begin_transaction`/
    27	//! `txn_add`/`commit_transaction`/`rollback_transaction`, inc.2c-5 — a pure
    28	//! `SessionOp` buffer that commits through the same `batch` machinery) +
    29	//! **`undo`/`redo`** (inc.2c-7) + **`.qbook` `open`/`save`** (inc.2c-9 — Option 1:
    30	//! reconstruct from the envelope, fresh op-log/undo history) + **xlsx `import`**
    31	//! (inc.2c-10 — same Option-1 adoption, via `ql_io_xlsx` + the injected
    32	//! `EngineXlsxRecomputer`) + **`csv` `import`/`export`** (inc.2c-11 — via the
    33	//! pure-I/O `ql_io_csv`; no recompute since CSV has no formulas) are also real.
    34	//! **xlsx `export`** (inc.2c-12 — whole-workbook, via `ql_io_xlsx` behind the
    35	//! `xlsx-write` feature so the default reader-only build stays lean; without
    36	//! the feature it returns the honest `Capability` error) is also real. The
    37	//! remaining contract methods return a **surfaced** `Capability` /
    38	//! `not_implemented_in_v1_core` error (NOT a silent fallback — project
    39	//! No-Fallbacks rule) and land in later sub-increments:
    40	//! - function registration (6.4), reserved bulk ops (6.4/6.5).
    41	//!
    42	//! ## Two design facts that shape this increment
    43	//!
    44	//! 1. **Version token = `{epoch, state_seq}` (inc.2c-3 — F1/F2).** `state_seq` is
    45	//!    a session-owned monotonic clock that advances on every committed mutation
    46	//!    AND every recompute that changed ≥1 cell. It **replaces `oplog.len()`** as
    47	//!    the token's counter because `oplog.len()` does NOT advance on recompute
    48	//!    (`put_computed_at` appends no ops), which would let two distinct visible
    49	//!    states share a token and make `snapshot_delta` silently under-report.
    50	//!    (Historical note: pre-F2, a `set_value(Blank)` clear also appended no op
    51	//!    — that gap is now closed by `Op::ClearValue`, but the recompute case
    52	//!    remains the load-bearing reason `state_seq` exists.) `ql-oplog::OpLog`
    53	//!    DOES expose a
    54	//!    Loro VV (`oplog_vv()`), reserved for the v1.5 collab delta-sync path; the
    55	//!    opaque 24-byte token shape is unchanged (only the counter's meaning is).
    56	//!    `snapshot_delta` walks a bounded change-log keyed by `state_seq`; the
    57	//!    `epoch` (bumped on reorder/restore/table-op/undo) forces a full rebuild for
    58	//!    states the delta DTO cannot incrementally express.
    59	//!
    60	//! 2. **`EngineError` cannot have `From<RuntimeError>`** — orphan rules forbid
    61	//!    `impl From<LocalErr> for ForeignErr` when the foreign type is `Self`
    62	//!    (`EngineError` is from `ql-session`). So the Appendix-A mapping lives as
    63	//!    the free function [`map_runtime_err`] in this owning layer, not as a
    64	//!    `From` impl.
    65	//!
    66	//! ## Read-path enumeration note
    67	//!
    68	//! `snapshot` enumerates a sheet's populated cells from the live `Workbook`'s
    69	//! **overlay** entries (user + computed) ∪ formula cells ∪ format-overlay cells.
    70	//! This is complete for every session constructible in this increment: all
    71	//! cell data arrives via `WorkbookRuntime` mutators, which write to the column
    72	//! *overlays* (never the Arrow *base* chunks). Base chunks are only populated
    73	//! by bulk construction / import (`open`/`import` — surfaced-not-yet here), so
    74	//! base-chunk enumeration is a tracked follow-up that lands with `import`.
    75	
