quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/ide-consumer-contract.md:1068:- **Entropy**: UUIDv4 has 122 random bits; truncating to 64 keeps ~64 bits.  Birthday-paradox collision probability is ~2^32 sessions before first collision -- effectively zero for real workbook usage.
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/ide-consumer-contract.md:1075:- ✅ **Closes R-V3.3-5 fully**: UUID 2^64 keyspace vs PID's ~2^15 effective range.
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/ide-consumer-contract.md:1172:- **R-V3.4-4 PeerId reuse across .qbook save/load** -- CLOSED at V3.4.0.4b via D5 DEVIATION (see § D5 above).  Fresh-UUID-per-session via `crypto.randomUUID` truncated to 64-bit BigInt.  R-V3.3-5 cross-restart PID collision carryforward also CLOSED (UUID 2^64 keyspace vs PID ~2^15 effective range).
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:20:1. **Existing coercion code is well-built but ad-hoc helpers have proliferated.** `ql-types::coercion` centralizes 5 public functions (`to_number_strict`, `to_number_lenient`, `to_logical`, `to_text_for_display`, `to_text_for_formula`, plus the `sanitize_f64` overflow leaf). However, three private helpers (`scalar_fns::coerce_numeric` (×2 — duplicated across `scalar_fns.rs` and `range_fns.rs`), `scalar_fns::coerce_text`, `scalar_fns::coerce_int_arg`) wrap the centralized fns to support function-arg patterns that the central module doesn't directly express (Blank-skip in aggregates, position/length integer extraction, text-result fallback). These helpers are correct individually but have drifted slightly between callers — e.g. the W5-60 audit caught that not all 2D-shape-aware fns handled `FnArg::Range` vs `FnArg::Scalar` consistently.
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:39:These are tested at 47 unit-test sites in `coercion::tests`. The contract is solid: Blank→0/false/"", Number passthrough (NaN/Inf→#NUM!), Bool→1/0 or TRUE/FALSE, Text strict/lenient as documented, Error propagates uniformly.
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:43:- `coerce_numeric(v: &Value) -> NumericArg` — wraps `to_number_strict` with `NumericArg::{Number, Skip, Error}` to distinguish Blank-skip in aggregates from Error.
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:45:- `coerce_int_arg(v: &Value) -> Result<i64, ErrorValue>` — for position/length args: Number truncates toward zero, Bool→0/1, Blank→0, Text→lenient-parse-truncate-or-#VALUE!, Error propagates.
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:65:| `Blank`           | `0.0`          | `0.0`            | `false`                       | `""`          | `Ok("")`     |
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:83:| Context              | Numeric coercion | Blank handling | Text handling                  | Error handling |
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:126:| `ISBLANK(v)`         | TRUE for Blank, FALSE everything else including errors. | [CURRENT][CANON] |
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:137:| `INDEX`              | row=0 / col=0 (array spill) → #REF! pending Phase 4.7 array formulas. Out-of-bounds → #REF!. | [CURRENT][V1-DIV — array via #REF!] |
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:139:| `LARGE / SMALL`      | Range-aware. Strict numeric — text in range → `#VALUE!`. k out of bounds → `#NUM!`. | [CURRENT][V1-DIV] |
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:159:3. **(Codex MEDIUM 3 fix)** Move `NumericArg` + `coerce_numeric` (the aggregate-with-skip variant) to `ql-types::coercion` as a **neutral** API: `pub fn to_number_strict_skip_blank(v: &Value) -> Result<Option<f64>, ErrorValue>`. `Ok(Some(n))` = Number, `Ok(None)` = Blank-skip, `Err(e)` = Error. This avoids leaking "aggregate" terminology into `ql-types` (which should remain function-agnostic). The function-specific `NumericArg` enum stays out of `ql-types`; if a caller still wants the match-friendly shape, it can `match` on the Result.
quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:232:- **Python/AI() boundary (Phase 6.4-6.6)** — Python UDF results map back to `Value` via a yet-to-be-defined boundary. The matrix should be the authority for "how a Python `None` / `numpy.nan` / `pandas.NA` becomes a Value." Pre-pin: `None` → `Blank`; `nan` → `Value::Error(#NUM!)`; `pd.NA` → `Blank` (TBD by Phase 6 entry).
