quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-29-6-4-3c-MEGAUDIT/lane-2-codexB-gpt55-xhigh.md:25:Explicit huge ranges and large produced arrays are materialized into `Vec<Value>`/Arrow payloads before the 64 MiB frame cap helps. Open-ended ranges clamp to populated sheet bounds, but bounded full-sheet ranges, huge named ranges, or large `SEQUENCE(...)` UDF args can allocate/iterate heavily on the synchronous recalc path.  
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-29-6-4-3c-MEGAUDIT/lane-2-codexB-gpt55-xhigh.md:38:CLEAN: spill writeback handles degenerate grids as `#CALC!`, out-of-bounds as `#SPILL!`, and blocks overwriting formulas/nonblank/spill targets before writing. 1x1 UDF results take the scalar path. Error-valued cells inside returned grids spill as normal values.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.G-sonnet-review.md:150:`ExprPlan::Array` matches `other` → `eval_scalar_with_cache` on it → `Value::Error(ErrorValue::Calc)` → `FnArg::Scalar(#CALC!)`. The range-aware function (e.g., SUMIF) receives a `#CALC!` scalar as its criteria-range — effectively a no-match criteria → result is `0` or an error depending on SUMIF's error-propagation logic.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.G-sonnet-review.md:186:`COUNTA` is in `is_aggregate_function` (plan.rs line 279) and the aggregate expansion fires. `counta()` counts all non-blank values: `c = args.iter().filter(|v| !matches!(v, Value::Blank)).count()`. Three values: `Number(1)`, `Text("")`, `Error(NA)` — none are `Blank` → count = 3. (Note: `""` is a `Text("")`, which is NOT `Blank` in the Quantbook type system. Blank is a distinct variant.)
