quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-29-6-4-3c-MEGAUDIT/lane-2-codexB-PROMPT.md:20:   `write_spill` path: spill collision, spill over a UDF's own arg cells, spill bounds, `#SPILL!`.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-11-codex.md:15:Detail: `extract_quartile_q` accepts `Value::Number(n)` and immediately returns `n.trunc() as i64`. That bypasses the finite-number guard used by the numeric range path (`collect_numbers_strict` -> `coerce_numeric` -> `to_number_strict_skip_blank` -> `sanitize_f64`) and also differs from the percentile `k` path, where NaN is rejected by the range containment check. In Rust, casting `f64::NAN.trunc()` to `i64` yields `0`, so `QUARTILE.INC(array, NaN)` would pass the `0..=4` check and return the minimum instead of an error. The same extraction also makes `quart` in `(-1, 0)` become `0`, so `QUARTILE.INC(array, -0.5)` returns the minimum; the prompt explicitly called out this truncation-vs-INT/bounds ambiguity as a Microsoft-compatibility risk.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-rt-step-4-opus.md:170:Per the global CLAUDE.md plan protocol: "Mark steps with checkboxes. Check them off as you complete work." Steps 2-3 are fully shipped per the cumulative summary; Step 4 is mid-ship. The plan file's checkboxes haven't tracked the shipped work — it's effectively a stale snapshot.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-rt-step-4-opus.md:367:**Detail:** `Workbook::put_formula` stores the text verbatim. Callers using it directly (instead of `WorkbookRuntime::set_formula`) get their raw text back from `formula_at` — and FORMULATEXT will prepend `=` to that raw text. If a caller passes `"=1+2"` (with leading `=`), FORMULATEXT returns `"==1+2"`. The doc-comment lists Phase 2A.13 bounds checks but says nothing about formula-text canonicalization or the FORMULATEXT interaction.
