quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.H-sonnet-review.md:104:**Gap 1 — boundary cell test (MEDIUM):** No test places an anchor near `MAX_ROW` / `MAX_COLUMN`. The design is explicit that bounds checking is the **runtime's** responsibility (§ 8.1 step b), but there is no test to document what the storage layer actually does when asked to register a spill that extends beyond the Excel grid. Concretely:
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.H-sonnet-review.md:110:The register loop computes `MAX_ROW + 1` as `u32` — no overflow (MAX_ROW = 1_048_575, u32::MAX = 4_294_967_295) — but it inserts a target entry for a logically out-of-bounds row. The storage layer silently accepts this. This is intentional per design, but a test documenting the behavior (even just asserting the registration succeeds and a target at `MAX_ROW+1` exists in the map) would lock in the "storage is bounds-agnostic" contract so a future reviewer doesn't add accidental defensive clamping.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.H-sonnet-review.md:112:**Citation:** `spill.rs:207–228` (register loop, no bounds check).
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.H-sonnet-review.md:124:- `clear_spill_at` clears computed overlays at every target ✓ — **this test implicitly covers the anchor cell**: the 1×3 spill at `(0,0,0)` writes computed at `(0,0,0)` (the anchor itself), `(0,0,1)`, `(0,0,2)` and asserts all three read `Blank` after `clear_spill_at`. The anchor overlay IS verified.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.H-sonnet-review.md:197:2. **MEDIUM — Missing boundary-cell storage test:** Add a test that documents the storage layer's behavior for an anchor near `MAX_ROW` / `MAX_COLUMN` (registration succeeds, target at `MAX_ROW+1` exists in map). This locks in the "storage is bounds-agnostic; runtime is responsible for bounds" contract so Phase 4.7.J knows exactly where the enforcement must go.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-28-6-1c-megaudit/SYNTHESIS.md:18:| D | Codex (gpt-5.5 xhigh, `read-only`) | `.qbook` persistence · xlsx/csv I/O · op-log payload integrity · effective-extent cross-cutting · `OPLOG_SCHEMA_VERSION` | `codex exec` | `lane-d.out` (10,698 lines) |
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-28-6-1c-megaudit/SYNTHESIS.md:60:| **M7** | D | **`Sheet::put` grows the conservative bounds even on `Value::Blank` writes** (`sheet.rs:155`); csv/xlsx/.qbook exporters all iterate `Sheet::bounds`, so a blank-inflated workbook produces a giant export. Lane D proposes a concrete fix (storage-level `Sheet::iter_effective_cells()` + `effective_value_bounds()` from `ColumnStore::iter_chunks()` + overlay ordering; do NOT shrink-on-`put(Blank)` because blank overlays are semantically meaningful masks). | `sheet.rs:155`; csv `lib.rs:244`; xlsx `umya_export.rs:436`; .qbook `qbook_format.rs:1402`. | **FILE as TRACKED CROSS-CUTTING (already in memory `current_work.md`).** Not a 6.1C blocker — not silent data loss; I/O scalability hazard with documented current behavior. Bundle with the inc.2c-12 INFO re HashMap-ordered fresh-sheet-rels (`umya_export.rs:755, :826`). Closes seed input #8. |
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-28-6-1c-megaudit/SYNTHESIS.md:68:| L2 | B | Epoch bump AFTER mutation, not BEFORE, for delta-inexpressible commands. Current single-thread + lock-held shape makes this effectively atomic to JS callers, but the documented temporal invariant ("bump before") isn't implemented. | `session.rs:1410, :1413, :1416, :1441, :1447, :1450, :1482, :1500`. | **FILE.** Not a correctness bug under v1 single-writer + mutex. Contract clarification: "epoch is bumped atomically with the mutation under the session lock" replaces "bumped before mutation". |
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-28-6-1c-megaudit/SYNTHESIS.md:99:| 8 | Cross-cutting: storage-level effective-non-blank extent API for csv/xlsx/.qbook serializers | **FILED CROSS-CUTTING as M7** with Lane D's concrete fix proposal. Already in `memory/current_work.md` tracker. |
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-28-6-1c-megaudit/SYNTHESIS.md:100:| 9 | `ops` + `events` unbounded growth | **FILED for 6.3 as M6.** Event-ring infrastructure per contract §9. `next_op_id → checked_add` could land in audit-fix; the bigger ops/events bounds need 6.3 work. |
