quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:8298:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-20-phase-5-2-d-1-step-4-codex.md:10770:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-16-phase-4.8.G.3-closing-megaudit-codex.md:2102:docs/architecture/2026-05-14-structured-references-and-tables.md:160:6. **Table footprint fits within the Excel address grid** — NEW W5-125 (Phase 4.8.O.1 closure, Codex HIGH-1). `top_row + rows - 1` must be `≤ MAX_ROW` (1,048,575) and `top_col + cols - 1` must be `≤ MAX_COLUMN` (16,383). `TableMetadata::validate_footprint_bounds` (in `ql-storage`) is the shared check; called at FIVE entry points: producer `create_table` / `resize_table`, replay `apply_create_table` / `apply_resize_table`, and v6 `.qbook` loader. Pre-W5-125 the check was missing, so `for r in top_row..top_row + rows` either ran past the addressable grid (registering cells later `Sheet::put` would panic on) or u32-overflowed silently (skipping all downstream overlap / spill-anchor checks).
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:8341:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.B-codex-review.txt:1966:docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:20:1. **Existing coercion code is well-built but ad-hoc helpers have proliferated.** `ql-types::coercion` centralizes 5 public functions (`to_number_strict`, `to_number_lenient`, `to_logical`, `to_text_for_display`, `to_text_for_formula`, plus the `sanitize_f64` overflow leaf). However, three private helpers (`scalar_fns::coerce_numeric` (×2 — duplicated across `scalar_fns.rs` and `range_fns.rs`), `scalar_fns::coerce_text`, `scalar_fns::coerce_int_arg`) wrap the centralized fns to support function-arg patterns that the central module doesn't directly express (Blank-skip in aggregates, position/length integer extraction, text-result fallback). These helpers are correct individually but have drifted slightly between callers — e.g. the W5-60 audit caught that not all 2D-shape-aware fns handled `FnArg::Range` vs `FnArg::Scalar` consistently.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:8384:quantbook-engine/docs/architecture/2026-05-13-coercion-matrix.md:20:1. **Existing coercion code is well-built but ad-hoc helpers have proliferated.** `ql-types::coercion` centralizes 5 public functions (`to_number_strict`, `to_number_lenient`, `to_logical`, `to_text_for_display`, `to_text_for_formula`, plus the `sanitize_f64` overflow leaf). However, three private helpers (`scalar_fns::coerce_numeric` (×2 — duplicated across `scalar_fns.rs` and `range_fns.rs`), `scalar_fns::coerce_text`, `scalar_fns::coerce_int_arg`) wrap the centralized fns to support function-arg patterns that the central module doesn't directly express (Blank-skip in aggregates, position/length integer extraction, text-result fallback). These helpers are correct individually but have drifted slightly between callers — e.g. the W5-60 audit caught that not all 2D-shape-aware fns handled `FnArg::Range` vs `FnArg::Scalar` consistently.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:8401:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-14-2-opus.md:34:- Why it matters: I ran 8 threads exporting to the same `/tmp/probe-corruption.xlsx` (Barrier-synced for max collision). Across 5 runs, **5-6 of 8 threads received WRONG output** every time. Each thread wrote a unique numeric sentinel to a unique column; on re-import, the sentinel was replaced with text/Blank values from a different thread's preservation source (basic_text.xlsx's shared-string content). This is silent data corruption: no error returned, output file looks valid, but cells contain another thread's content. The ScopedFileGuard then races on cleanup — `remove_file` of a nonexistent file is silently ignored.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:8408:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-14-2-opus.md:68:- Why it matters: Excel allows `'` in sheet names (escaped as `''`). The fallback path captures the raw text as `Formula(...)`, which the binder MAY resolve later (Phase 3+). But until the binder lands, names targeting `O'Brien!$A$1` are effectively orphaned (no Cell/Range resolution). The export side handles the escape correctly (`quote_sheet_name` does `name.replace('\'', "''")`); only the import side is asymmetric.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:8416:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-15-opus.md:25:### H-1: Blank-cell format overlays are silently lost on export
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:8422:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-15-opus.md:241:### M-2: Out-of-bounds xf index on import silently dropped without warning
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:8560:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-rt-step-2-opus.md:95:**(2) Read errors silently coerced to non-error refs.** WorkbookEnv's `read_cell` returns `Value::Error(ErrorValue::Ref)` for out-of-bounds sheet (env.rs:178-181, Phase 2A.7 audit H6 closure). The fallthrough `other` arm in materialize_ref_arg_eager at scalar.rs:618-624 handles this case:
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:8695:quantbook-engine/crates/ql-storage/src/column.rs:487:    // -- FIX-2: N7 + F4 — non-Float64 base panics, not silent Blank --------
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9189:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7-design-codex-review.txt:1475:    // -- FIX-2: N7 + F4 — non-Float64 base panics, not silent Blank --------
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9235:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-7-opus.md:178:The iterative kernel is the cleanest of the Wave 3 iterative paths (W5-D-4 BINOM.INV and W5-D-5 GAMMA.INV both produced Codex HIGHs around iteration bounds). XIRR's three-layer fallback ladder (Newton-from-guess → bisection → Newton-from-200) is bounded on every layer, and the `is_finite()` short-circuit makes adversarial paths exit early rather than chase NaN/inf around the function space. The added `f_prime == 0` guard in `xirr_newton` is a strict improvement over IronCalc.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9239:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-7-codex.md:39:- **Severity rationale:** Low because the implementation paths exist and the loop bounds prevent hangs, but future edits could break fallback behavior while the current suite remains green.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9357:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2633:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:983:docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-7-codex.md:39:- **Severity rationale:** Low because the implementation paths exist and the loop bounds prevent hangs, but future edits could break fallback behavior while the current suite remains green.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9360:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2690:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:1061:docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.H-sonnet-review.md:110:The register loop computes `MAX_ROW + 1` as `u32` — no overflow (MAX_ROW = 1_048_575, u32::MAX = 4_294_967_295) — but it inserts a target entry for a logically out-of-bounds row. The storage layer silently accepts this. This is intentional per design, but a test documenting the behavior (even just asserting the registration succeeds and a target at `MAX_ROW+1` exists in the map) would lock in the "storage is bounds-agnostic" contract so a future reviewer doesn't add accidental defensive clamping.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9361:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2693:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:1064:docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-12-opus.md:104:- **Detail:** Both engine `scalar_fns::counta` and IronCalc `subtotal_counta` count errors (engine: `!matches!(v, Value::Blank).count()` includes Error; IronCalc: `String | Number | Boolean | Error => counta += 1`). Behavior matches. **However**, this is NOT pinned by any SUBTOTAL test — the `subtotal_counta_function_num_3` test only covers `[1, "skip", 2, blank, 3]` (no error). A future refactor of `scalar_fns::counta` to skip errors would silently break compatibility with IronCalc + Excel canon (Excel COUNTA does count errors) and the engine-wide aggregate convention. Probe-verified: `SUBTOTAL(3, [1, #DIV/0!, 3]) = ?`. **Not tested** in the new SUBTOTAL block (probe-confirmed = 3.0 — passes through to counta which counts the error).
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9413:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:3572:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:983:docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-7-codex.md:39:- **Severity rationale:** Low because the implementation paths exist and the loop bounds prevent hangs, but future edits could break fallback behavior while the current suite remains green.
