quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9807:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:72:### H4 — v1→v2 migration rule silently corrupts legitimate `Error("#NULL!") + formula` cells into Blank
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9808:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:77:Both encode identically in v1. The migration silently rewrites the second case to `Value::Blank`, losing the user's actual error result.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9811:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:96:**Agent C finding.** H6 was closed at the evaluator layer (`env.rs:38-51`). But `Workbook::read` (`workbook.rs:260-265`) — the public storage read API — still maps missing-sheet to `Blank`. Production formula eval correctly returns `#REF!`; direct public read returns the silent fallback.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:9818:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.H-sonnet-review.md:110:The register loop computes `MAX_ROW + 1` as `u32` — no overflow (MAX_ROW = 1_048_575, u32::MAX = 4_294_967_295) — but it inserts a target entry for a logically out-of-bounds row. The storage layer silently accepts this. This is intentional per design, but a test documenting the behavior (even just asserting the registration succeeds and a target at `MAX_ROW+1` exists in the map) would lock in the "storage is bounds-agnostic" contract so a future reviewer doesn't add accidental defensive clamping.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:10166:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-20-phase-5-3-step-4-codex.md:7615:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-16-phase-4.8.G.3-closing-megaudit-codex.md:1595:crates/ql-exec/src/env.rs:430:    /// not `Blank`. (Was previously a silent Phase-3-deferred fallback.)
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:10170:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-20-phase-5-3-step-4-codex.md:7748:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-16-phase-4.8.G.3-closing-megaudit-codex.md:2243:docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit.md:87:A formula authored against `Sheet3!A1` in a workbook now with 2 sheets binds to `ExprPlan::CellRef { sheet: 2, ... }`, which `WorkbookEnv::read_cell` maps to `Value::Blank`. The env.rs comment admits this is a deferred semantic ("Phase 3+ FormulaRegion binder"). Two agents flagged it. The same agents flagged that `Phase 2A.6` already established the pattern (`NamedTargetIsBlank`, `NamedTargetIsError`) for surfacing previously-silent paths.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:10172:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-20-phase-5-3-step-4-codex.md:8010:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit.md:87:A formula authored against `Sheet3!A1` in a workbook now with 2 sheets binds to `ExprPlan::CellRef { sheet: 2, ... }`, which `WorkbookEnv::read_cell` maps to `Value::Blank`. The env.rs comment admits this is a deferred semantic ("Phase 3+ FormulaRegion binder"). Two agents flagged it. The same agents flagged that `Phase 2A.6` already established the pattern (`NamedTargetIsBlank`, `NamedTargetIsError`) for surfacing previously-silent paths.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:10299:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-12-opus.md:104:- **Detail:** Both engine `scalar_fns::counta` and IronCalc `subtotal_counta` count errors (engine: `!matches!(v, Value::Blank).count()` includes Error; IronCalc: `String | Number | Boolean | Error => counta += 1`). Behavior matches. **However**, this is NOT pinned by any SUBTOTAL test — the `subtotal_counta_function_num_3` test only covers `[1, "skip", 2, blank, 3]` (no error). A future refactor of `scalar_fns::counta` to skip errors would silently break compatibility with IronCalc + Excel canon (Excel COUNTA does count errors) and the engine-wide aggregate convention. Probe-verified: `SUBTOTAL(3, [1, #DIV/0!, 3]) = ?`. **Not tested** in the new SUBTOTAL block (probe-confirmed = 3.0 — passes through to counta which counts the error).
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:10355:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:719:docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-7-opus.md:178:The iterative kernel is the cleanest of the Wave 3 iterative paths (W5-D-4 BINOM.INV and W5-D-5 GAMMA.INV both produced Codex HIGHs around iteration bounds). XIRR's three-layer fallback ladder (Newton-from-guess → bisection → Newton-from-200) is bounded on every layer, and the `is_finite()` short-circuit makes adversarial paths exit early rather than chase NaN/inf around the function space. The added `f_prime == 0` guard in `xirr_newton` is a strict improvement over IronCalc.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:10370:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:983:docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-7-codex.md:39:- **Severity rationale:** Low because the implementation paths exist and the loop bounds prevent hangs, but future edits could break fallback behavior while the current suite remains green.
