quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:10969:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-15-self.md:108:### M-2: Out-of-bounds xf index on import silently skips with no warning
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:11033:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-2-b-codex.md:4066:docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:96:**Agent C finding.** H6 was closed at the evaluator layer (`env.rs:38-51`). But `Workbook::read` (`workbook.rs:260-265`) — the public storage read API — still maps missing-sheet to `Blank`. Production formula eval correctly returns `#REF!`; direct public read returns the silent fallback.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:11122:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-1-opus.md:98:**Severity rationale:** HIGH because (a) Boolean-in-numeric-position is a frequent real-world arg pattern (`=NORM.DIST(A1, mean, sd, TRUE)` where `A1 = TRUE` for a sentinel column) — silent behavioral divergence from the port source is the worst kind of bug because tests pass and users see no error; (b) the project's documented strategy in module docstring is "port IronCalc verbatim … numerical results are identical out-of-the-box" — this statement is **strictly false** under any non-Number/Blank arg, and the docstring should not assert it; (c) this is forward-looking for the rest of Wave 3, which will all want to inherit a consistent policy. Decide now or pay later.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:11126:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-1-opus.md:196:- `NORM.DIST(1, Blank, 1, TRUE)` would compute `Φ(1) ≈ 0.8413` (silently treats missing mean as 0).
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:11301:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-16-phase-4.8.G.3-closing-megaudit-codex.md:1595:crates/ql-exec/src/env.rs:430:    /// not `Blank`. (Was previously a silent Phase-3-deferred fallback.)
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:11305:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-16-phase-4.8.G.3-closing-megaudit-codex.md:2102:docs/architecture/2026-05-14-structured-references-and-tables.md:160:6. **Table footprint fits within the Excel address grid** — NEW W5-125 (Phase 4.8.O.1 closure, Codex HIGH-1). `top_row + rows - 1` must be `≤ MAX_ROW` (1,048,575) and `top_col + cols - 1` must be `≤ MAX_COLUMN` (16,383). `TableMetadata::validate_footprint_bounds` (in `ql-storage`) is the shared check; called at FIVE entry points: producer `create_table` / `resize_table`, replay `apply_create_table` / `apply_resize_table`, and v6 `.qbook` loader. Pre-W5-125 the check was missing, so `for r in top_row..top_row + rows` either ran past the addressable grid (registering cells later `Sheet::put` would panic on) or u32-overflowed silently (skipping all downstream overlap / spill-anchor checks).
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-22-phase-5-7-v2-2-codex.md:11311:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-16-phase-4.8.G.3-closing-megaudit-codex.md:2243:docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit.md:87:A formula authored against `Sheet3!A1` in a workbook now with 2 sheets binds to `ExprPlan::CellRef { sheet: 2, ... }`, which `WorkbookEnv::read_cell` maps to `Value::Blank`. The env.rs comment admits this is a deferred semantic ("Phase 3+ FormulaRegion binder"). Two agents flagged it. The same agents flagged that `Phase 2A.6` already established the pattern (`NamedTargetIsBlank`, `NamedTargetIsError`) for surfacing previously-silent paths.
