quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-14-codex.md:103:**Detail:** `id`, `headerRowCount`, `totalsRowCount`, and `tableColumn/@id` parse failures fall back to `0`/defaults. The parser does not validate `tableColumns/@count`, column count vs `ref` width, duplicate/zero column ids, duplicate table names, or table footprint bounds. `TableTable::insert` overwrites on canonical-name collision, so malformed duplicate tables silently lose one table.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-14-codex.md:105:**Recommendation:** Treat malformed numeric required attributes as `MalformedOoxml`, validate footprint with `TableMetadata::validate_footprint_bounds`, reject duplicate canonical table names, and validate column roster shape before insertion.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-14-codex.md:183:**Subject:** NewWorkbook export is dense over sheet bounds and can hang on sparse far-boundary cells
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-14-codex.md:187:**Detail:** The exporter iterates every row/column in `sheet.bounds()`. A workbook with a single populated cell at `MAX_ROW, MAX_COLUMN` forces roughly 17 billion loop iterations. This can occur from a valid sparse XLSX boundary cell and makes export effectively non-terminating.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-14-codex.md:189:**Recommendation:** Iterate sparse column/cell storage rather than rectangular bounds, and separately merge formula-only cells. Add a boundary-cell export test that completes quickly.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-18-w5-d-14-codex.md:306:- The Quantbook-to-umya coordinate conversion (`col + 1`, `row + 1`) is correct for the storage bounds.
