quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-13-w5-63-codex-coercion-prompt.md:45:4. Excel-canon: `Value::Blank` in numeric context coerces to 0; in logical context to FALSE; in text context to "". Confirmed in our coercion module. But Excel ALSO treats `Value::Blank` differently in COUNT vs COUNTA (COUNT skips, COUNTA counts). The matrix doesn't capture this — should it?
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-phase-4-10-megaudit-opus.md:233:`HEX2DEC` and `OCT2DEC` both call `collect_base_decode_arg` which accepts Text, Number (stringifies with `format!("{:.0}", n)`), Boolean ("TRUE"/"FALSE"), and Blank (""). `BIN2DEC` calls `coercion::to_number_strict` directly which REJECTS Text (returns `ErrorValue::Value`).
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-phase-4-10-megaudit-opus.md:235:The W5-D-9.1 Opus LOW-3 closure note (`scalar_fns.rs:3638-3642`) explicitly says: "Excel canon: auto-coerce Number (via integer rendering), Boolean (TRUE/FALSE), Blank ("")". The closure was applied to HEX2DEC and OCT2DEC but NOT BIN2DEC, creating the asymmetry.
