quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:38:| `Workbook::read` still returns Blank for missing sheet — H6 fixed eval path, not read API |   |   |   | ✓ |
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:63:**Codex finding.** `Workbook::put_formula` (`workbook.rs:293`) accepts any (sheet, row, col) without bounds checking. The 2A.7 H1 fix added `validate_cell` at the runtime/transaction layer, but a caller reaching directly into `Workbook::put_formula` (the qbook loader is the prod caller) bypasses it. Downstream `recompute_all`'s `put_at` would panic on bad coords.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:65:**Fix**: make `Workbook::put_formula` return `Result<(), InvalidCell>` and validate row/col internally. Or document the invariant + assert at debug-build time. Loader already pre-validates row/col bounds (qbook_format.rs:911-924), so this is defense-in-depth + future-proofing.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:72:### H4 — v1→v2 migration rule silently corrupts legitimate `Error("#NULL!") + formula` cells into Blank
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:74:- (intended) v1 engine's encoding of formula-bearing Blank cells.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:77:Both encode identically in v1. The migration silently rewrites the second case to `Value::Blank`, losing the user's actual error result.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:95:### M2 — `Workbook::read` returns `Blank` for missing sheet; inconsistent with `WorkbookEnv::read_cell`'s `#REF!`
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:96:**Agent C finding.** H6 was closed at the evaluator layer (`env.rs:38-51`). But `Workbook::read` (`workbook.rs:260-265`) — the public storage read API — still maps missing-sheet to `Blank`. Production formula eval correctly returns `#REF!`; direct public read returns the silent fallback.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:120:### M7 — `NamedTargetWire::Constant { value: Pending }` conflates "named-Blank constant" with "formula-pending"
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:121:**Agent A finding.** Save path emits `Pending` for `NamedTarget::Constant(Value::Blank)` (via `from_value` returning None → unwrap_or Pending). Load round-trips Pending back to Blank correctly today, but the wire format provides no semantic distinction between "intentionally Blank named constant" and "formula-bearing cell not yet evaluated." Any future change to Pending semantics breaks the named-constant case.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:123:**Fix**: add `CellWireValue::Blank` as a distinct wire variant, OR refuse to serialize `NamedTarget::Constant(Blank)` outright (degenerate case).
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:149:- **L6**: `compare_values_excel` missing `Blank vs Bool` test (Agent A + B). One test, ~5 lines.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-9-opus.md:109:  Same applies to Blank: IronCalc treats EmptyCell as "" text → parse-fail → `#NUM!`. Ours returns `#VALUE!` (Blank is not Text → match-fall-through).
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-9-opus.md:111:  (a) **Match canon**: change to `coercion::to_text_for_arg` (which handles Number/Bool/Blank/Text/Error uniformly). This would auto-fix Number → coerce to "10" → parse as hex/octal → 16/8 respectively. Boolean → "TRUE"/"FALSE" → parse fails → `#NUM!`. Blank → "" → parse fails → `#NUM!`. ALL three align with IronCalc/Excel. Trade-off: changes the `_number_arg_is_value_error` tests.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-9-opus.md:112:  (b) **Document divergence**: leave behavior, but rewrite the docstring from "per Excel canon" to "**deliberate divergence from Excel/IronCalc**: takes Text-only; Number/Boolean/Blank → `#VALUE!`. Rationale: project-wide tighter-than-Excel arg-typing for engineering fns."
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-phase-2B.md:103:**Disposition:** **FIXED** in this audit-closure commit. New `_assert_engine_state_is_send_sync` compile-time check in `ql-exec/src/lib.rs`. Today's Loro 1.12.0 LoroDoc satisfies both bounds; the check pins the invariant going forward.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-12-phase-2B.md:133:- **`clear_formula` Blank-skip shadowed in GAP-O-04 doc** (Correctness D8): added explicit note that the GAP applies to `clear_formula` too.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-reference-tier-design-codex.md:83:**Detail:** In the D2 design, every argument to a ReferenceAware function is materialized by source-plan shape. The sketch never creates `RefArg::Scalar`; non-reference expressions become `Literal`. So `Scalar` is effectively unused for this mini-phase. Meanwhile `Literal(Value)` is overloaded: it means non-reference literal, arithmetic expression, function result, and error result. That overloading causes HIGH-2 and HIGH-7.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-reference-tier-design-codex.md:99:**Detail:** The design says `ROWS(A:A)` should return the workbook row limit, but the materializer sketch calls `read_range_with_shape` for every range. `WorkbookEnv::read_range_with_shape` intentionally clamps open-ended ranges to populated bounds for aggregate scans (`env.rs:222`). The coordinate fields can still produce the right answer if copied from the original `Range`, but materializing values is unnecessary and can be expensive for large populated ranges. It also risks later implementers using the clamped `rows/cols` instead of the authored range bounds.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-reference-tier-design-codex.md:101:**Recommendation:** For this mini-phase, make range reference materialization coordinate-only by default. Add values lazily only for a function that actually iterates. Pin `ROWS(A:A) = 1_048_576` and `COLUMNS(1:1) = 16_384` independent of populated sheet bounds.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-reference-tier-design-codex.md:123:**Detail:** Six tests per function is not enough for a new binder/eval/storage tier. The plan bundles cross-sheet, named range, and range variants into one slot, and does not require structured refs, implicit intersection, array constants, error propagation, formula text canonicalization, or whole-row/whole-column bounds.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-reference-tier-design-codex.md:125:**Recommendation:** Add a tier-level matrix: literal ranges, named ranges, cross-sheet refs, structured refs (`Sales[Qty]` and `[@Qty]`), `@` implicit intersection, array constants for ROWS/COLUMNS, max bounds, #REF propagation, formula text with leading `=`, non-formula `#N/A`, and recompute/dirty behavior for ISFORMULA/FORMULATEXT.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-handoff-completeness-audit-codex.md:1119:    28	| ~~GAP-B-01~~ | ~~Named-range BIND surface for aggregate context~~ — **FULLY CLOSED** in Engine Phase 3.6 (W5-39, commit-after-18b19cbc4b5). Phase 2B.4 added the bind shape (`ExprPlan::AggregateNameRef`); Phase 3.6 adds the actual evaluator: SUM/AVERAGE/MIN/MAX/COUNT/PRODUCT over a single named-range arg now compute the real aggregate (via `CellEnv::read_range` clamped to `Sheet::bounds`) instead of `#CALC!`. Cache + invalidation also shipped (AGG-3-01..04). |
