quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2285:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-rt-step-2-opus.md:127:**Reachability assessment.** Today, the workbook_runtime has no `delete_sheet` API (grep for `delete_sheet` / `remove_sheet` in workbook_runtime.rs returns 0 production paths). So the deleted-sheet case isn't exercisable from end-user formulas. BUT: any future deletion API will need to either (a) bring this in scope as a regression OR (b) ship with this materializer arm pre-fixed. Also: the WorkbookEnv's `read_cell` returns `Blank` for missing cells in an existing sheet — that's correct and not an error. The error-path is reachable only via the missing-sheet branch today.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2301:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-17-rt-step-2-opus.md:127:**Reachability assessment.** Today, the workbook_runtime has no `delete_sheet` API (grep for `delete_sheet` / `remove_sheet` in workbook_runtime.rs returns 0 production paths). So the deleted-sheet case isn't exercisable from end-user formulas. BUT: any future deletion API will need to either (a) bring this in scope as a regression OR (b) ship with this materializer arm pre-fixed. Also: the WorkbookEnv's `read_cell` returns `Blank` for missing cells in an existing sheet — that's correct and not an error. The error-path is reachable only via the missing-sheet branch today.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2618:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:935:docs/audits/2026-05-17-rt-step-2-opus.md:127:**Reachability assessment.** Today, the workbook_runtime has no `delete_sheet` API (grep for `delete_sheet` / `remove_sheet` in workbook_runtime.rs returns 0 production paths). So the deleted-sheet case isn't exercisable from end-user formulas. BUT: any future deletion API will need to either (a) bring this in scope as a regression OR (b) ship with this materializer arm pre-fixed. Also: the WorkbookEnv's `read_cell` returns `Blank` for missing cells in an existing sheet — that's correct and not an error. The error-path is reachable only via the missing-sheet branch today.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2633:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:983:docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-7-codex.md:39:- **Severity rationale:** Low because the implementation paths exist and the loop bounds prevent hangs, but future edits could break fallback behavior while the current suite remains green.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2690:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:1061:docs/audits/2026-05-14-phase-4.7.H-sonnet-review.md:110:The register loop computes `MAX_ROW + 1` as `u32` — no overflow (MAX_ROW = 1_048_575, u32::MAX = 4_294_967_295) — but it inserts a target entry for a logically out-of-bounds row. The storage layer silently accepts this. This is intentional per design, but a test documenting the behavior (even just asserting the registration succeeds and a target at `MAX_ROW+1` exists in the map) would lock in the "storage is bounds-agnostic" contract so a future reviewer doesn't add accidental defensive clamping.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2693:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:1064:docs/audits/2026-05-17-w5-d-12-opus.md:104:- **Detail:** Both engine `scalar_fns::counta` and IronCalc `subtotal_counta` count errors (engine: `!matches!(v, Value::Blank).count()` includes Error; IronCalc: `String | Number | Boolean | Error => counta += 1`). Behavior matches. **However**, this is NOT pinned by any SUBTOTAL test — the `subtotal_counta_function_num_3` test only covers `[1, "skip", 2, blank, 3]` (no error). A future refactor of `scalar_fns::counta` to skip errors would silently break compatibility with IronCalc + Excel canon (Excel COUNTA does count errors) and the engine-wide aggregate convention. Probe-verified: `SUBTOTAL(3, [1, #DIV/0!, 3]) = ?`. **Not tested** in the new SUBTOTAL block (probe-confirmed = 3.0 — passes through to counta which counts the error).
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2866:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:1242:docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:65:**Fix**: make `Workbook::put_formula` return `Result<(), InvalidCell>` and validate row/col internally. Or document the invariant + assert at debug-build time. Loader already pre-validates row/col bounds (qbook_format.rs:911-924), so this is defense-in-depth + future-proofing.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2867:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:1243:docs/audits/2026-05-12-megaudit-cycle3.md:121:**Agent A finding.** Save path emits `Pending` for `NamedTarget::Constant(Value::Blank)` (via `from_value` returning None → unwrap_or Pending). Load round-trips Pending back to Blank correctly today, but the wire format provides no semantic distinction between "intentionally Blank named constant" and "formula-bearing cell not yet evaluated." Any future change to Pending semantics breaks the named-constant case.
quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-4-v1-codex.md:2982:quantbook-engine/docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-6-v1-codex.md:1470:docs/audits/2026-05-19-phase-5-2-b-codex.md:3865:docs/audits/2026-05-17-rt-step-2-opus.md:127:**Reachability assessment.** Today, the workbook_runtime has no `delete_sheet` API (grep for `delete_sheet` / `remove_sheet` in workbook_runtime.rs returns 0 production paths). So the deleted-sheet case isn't exercisable from end-user formulas. BUT: any future deletion API will need to either (a) bring this in scope as a regression OR (b) ship with this materializer arm pre-fixed. Also: the WorkbookEnv's `read_cell` returns `Blank` for missing cells in an existing sheet — that's correct and not an error. The error-path is reachable only via the missing-sheet branch today.
